C=cite
T=[]
T.append("# How has corruption, or allegations of corruption, affected U.S. foreign policy decisions or the conduct of diplomacy?\n")
T.append("*Research run s5, 2026-10-30. Every count below is conditional on the local index, which holds all 553 volumes of the series but only the documents it was able to parse (321,749), and is thin outside 1860 to 1989. The exact figures and the queries behind them are in the memo.*\n")
T.append("## The bottom line\n")
T.append(f"**1. What FRUS answers directly.** FRUS answers this question directly but unevenly. About three thousand documents (roughly one in a hundred of the 311,000 documents in the 551 first-edition volumes, once front matter, editorial notes and two duplicate second editions are set aside) use the vocabulary of corruption, bribery or graft. Most only describe a foreign government. On a forty-document test, eight of the forty tie the word, in the document's own terms, to something Americans chose or did, about one in five. Those choices fall into four kinds. First, Americans kept backing a government they called corrupt and tried to supervise it rather than drop it: China from 1944 to 1949 ({C('frus1944v06/d269')}), Greece in 1947 ({C('frus1947v05/d23')}) and the Philippines in 1985 ({C('frus1981-88v01/d247')}). Second, they made a loan or aid depend on reform: Cuba in 1922 ({C('frus1922v01/d864')}), the Philippines in 1950 ({C('frus1950v06/d802')}), and a recipient, Burma, declined American money in 1955 for the same reason ({C('frus1955-57v22/d7')}). Third, in Vietnam they declined to press an ally hard enough to change it, because pressing meant taking over its government ({C('frus1964-68v05/d258')}). Fourth, from 1975, the payer was American, and Washington regulated and negotiated over its own firms' bribery ({C('frus1969-76ve09p2/d155')}; {C('frus1977-80v24/d337')}). The documents say the corruption mattered to American choices; they seldom say it was the reason for one.\n")
T.append("**2. Where FRUS points for the rest.** FRUS is a selection, so two channels lead to the unprinted record, and they are kept apart here. Of the 38 documents in the collection, 37 carry a source line and one, an 1893 despatch, does not. The channel of *where the printed documents came from* is:\n")
T.append("- General Records of the Department of State, Record Group 59, the Central Files (numbered decimal files before 1963, later subject-numeric files): 23 of the 37. The offline data does not give them a National Archives identifier, so the entry, dates and access must come from the National Archives' reference staff.\n- Conference Files, Executive Secretariat, Record Group 59, entry A1 3051B, National Archives identifier 602875, 1949 to 1972, 238 linear feet, unrestricted: one document, from the lot (a Department filing unit; the editors cite it as 59 D 95). The match is by the series' name, not by a shared control number, so treat it as probable.\n- The Policy Planning Staff director's correspondence, lot 89 D 149, Record Group 59, 1981 to 1988: two documents. The offline data names the lot but has no National Archives identifier or entry for it.\n- Presidential libraries: Carter (the National Security Affairs files, five documents), Eisenhower (the Whitman File, one) and Reagan (the Rosenberg Files, one). The offline catalogue resolves none of them to an identifier; access is not stated.\n- Not National Archives holdings: the Library of Congress's Kissinger Papers (two documents), the Army Center of Military History's files of the American civilian-pacification command in Vietnam, folder \"GVN Corruption 1967\" (one), and a State Department Johnson Files series (one).\n")
T.append("The channel of *what the editors cited in footnotes and did not print*, counted over the roughly three thousand documents that use the vocabulary, points mainly to presidential libraries and to Department lot files. By count it runs: Carter Library National Security Affairs (53 citations), Johnson Library National Security File (28), Nixon Library NSC Files (27), then two Executive Secretariat lots, 66 D 95 (17) and 63 D 351 (12). Both lots belong to one series, Records Relating to National Security Council Policy Papers, Record Group 59, entry A1 1586E, National Archives identifier 2839192, 1947 to 1979, partly restricted (national-security exemption). The same footnotes also cite 450 decimal-file classes. This channel has no entry before December 1910, stores only the citation fragment, and holds lot, library and decimal-file anchors only. The offline data barely reaches before 1940, so the thin showing for earlier years is a limit of the tools, not of the archives.\n")
T.append("**3. What this request cannot get from FRUS.** (a) A rate. Nothing printed lets anyone say how often corruption decided an American choice: FRUS prints a selection, and the documents that use the words mostly describe a foreign regime. I searched the corruption vocabulary (corrupt, bribe, graft, embezzle, kickback, and \"illicit\" or \"questionable\" payments) across all 551 first-edition volumes, with a positive control (\"Department of State\", found in 94,049 documents and in every volume but one Paris Peace Conference volume) and a negative control (an impossible string, found nowhere). (b) Whether the stated reason was the real one. What Nu, the Shah or Diem said about corruption reaches us through American reporters; the shelf is the foreign archives, which the offline data does not index. (c) The years outside the index's strength. The index holds 143 dated documents for the 1840s and 158 for the 1850s, and for the 1990s only 177, from one volume: none of those 177 uses the vocabulary, though 130 of them use \"Department of State\", so the silence is the index's limit. (d) The private side of the bribery. FRUS prints the Department's reaction to the Senate subcommittee's disclosures about Northrop and Lockheed, not the companies' own records; the shelf is the subcommittee's and the Securities and Exchange Commission's files, which the offline data does not locate. (e) Intelligence reporting on foreign corruption: the Central Intelligence Agency appears as the source of 117 of the hit documents, and its files are outside the offline data.\n")
T.append("**Scope.** The collection holds 38 documents from 27 volumes, dated 1893 to 1986, each one the argument rests on; the search behind it covered about 311,000 documents. It excludes front matter and editorial notes, and the second editions of two volumes (Iran 1951 to 1954 and one 1969 to 1976 electronic volume) whose first editions are also in the index.\n")
T.append("## Decisions this needs from you\n")
T.append("- **What counts as corruption.** I used official or commercial corruption, bribery, graft and allegations of them, in a partner government, in American hands, or hurled at Americans. A narrower rule (corruption of foreign officials only) would drop the 1975 to 1980 payments finding; a wider one (any misrule, nepotism, \"squeeze\" as an everyday cost) would add many documents about Chinese and Levantine courts and customs and is not counted here. Inclusion rule used meanwhile: the definition in the first sentence of this item.\n- **What counts as affecting a decision.** Inclusion rule used meanwhile: I required that a document tie corruption, in its own words, to an American choice: aid, a loan, supervision, jurisdiction, a démarche, a treaty or a reporting requirement. If you also count corruption that merely formed the background to a decision, the set grows from about one in five to nearly all of the 3,058 hit documents, and the findings below become a survey, not an argument.\n- **How far back to go.** Inclusion rule used meanwhile: I treated the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries as in scope because corruption was then a stated reason for American practice (consular courts, loan supervision). If you want only the Cold War, drop the first and last findings and the Cuba and Nicaragua documents.\n- **Whether a recipient's own decision counts.** Inclusion rule used meanwhile: yes. The Burma refusal of 1955 is a decision by the other government that American money was not wanted; I kept it because the embassy reported it as a limit on American aid. Dropping it changes no other finding.\n")
T.append("## Findings\n")
T.append(f"### 1. Corruption was diagnosed early and often, and the usual response was supervision, not withdrawal\n\nIn February 1944 Ambassador Gauss reported that criticism of Chinese corruption did not reach the Generalissimo himself except for his failure to remove the corrupt: {q('frus1944v06/d269')} ({C('frus1944v06/d269')}). In 1947 a member of the Chinese State Council told an American embassy officer, for General Wedemeyer, that aid should come with close supervision ({C('frus1947v07/d553')}); the same year Under Secretary Acheson listed corruption among the reasons Greek and outside aid could not be used well, and in the next sentences asked for American and British experts in public administration: {q('frus1947v05/d23')} ({C('frus1947v05/d23')}). In November 1948 Chinese legislators complained, as Ambassador Stuart reported, that Communist propaganda had led the Americans to think the National Government corrupt ({C('frus1948v07/d440')}). By August 1949 the Chinese Acting President's own circle asked Americans to {q('frus1949v08/d576')} ({C('frus1949v08/d576')}). The pattern returns in 1985, when the Policy Planning Staff told the Secretary of State {q('frus1981-88v01/d247')} ({C('frus1981-88v01/d247')}) and warned that President Marcos might be a liability; in March 1986 the Bureau of Intelligence and Research listed corruption among the usual causes of a regime's exhausted legitimacy, {q('frus1981-88v01/d264')} ({C('frus1981-88v01/d264')}). In the documents cited here corruption is a reason to doubt a client and to ask for control or reform; none gives it alone as the reason to end support.\n")
T.append(f"### 2. Aid and loans were made to depend on reform, and the demand was easier to make than to enforce\n\nGeneral Crowder, the President's special representative in Cuba, wrote in April 1922 that he could not produce proof of the graft he was asked to stop: {q('frus1922v01/d841')} ({C('frus1922v01/d841')}). His memoranda came in a planned sequence, the budget first (April 1922, {C('frus1922v01/d836')}), then graft, then the national lottery, whose corruption he called beyond Cuban remedy ({C('frus1922v01/d842')}). By October 1922 the Department's Latin American office reported that he thought Washington should refuse a loan until the legislature's amnesty for indicted lottery officials was killed: {q('frus1922v01/d864')} ({C('frus1922v01/d864')}); the Department's September cable to him had already called the reform plan vital for stamping out corruption ({C('frus1922v01/d862')}). In February 1950 Acheson told Truman, before President Quirino's visit, that {q('frus1950v06/d802')} ({C('frus1950v06/d802')}); in 1952 the Manila ambassador proposed to tell Quirino privately that aid would stop if the next election was stolen ({C('frus1952-54v12p2/d324')}). The pressure ran in other directions too: in May 1955 Prime Minister Nu told the American embassy he leaned against a loan because corruption made it impossible to ensure that funds were properly used ({C('frus1955-57v22/d7')}), in June 1964 the embassy in Santo Domingo proposed that Mann tell the Dominican leader Bonilla that the aid program would lose value if run amid corruption ({C('frus1964-68v32/d8')}), and in May 1963 Secretary of State Rusk asked Belgian Foreign Minister Spaak what could be done to control corruption in Leopoldville, adding that Congress would give trouble over aid to the Congo if corruption was widespread ({C('frus1961-63v20/d422')}). The earliest case in the set, from 1912, describes the Zelaya years as {q('frus1912/d1502')} in urging the Senate to approve the Nicaragua loan convention ({C('frus1912/d1502')}).\n")
T.append(f"### 3. In Vietnam the question was how far an ally could be pressed\n\nIn April 1960 the Secretary of State relayed to Saigon a conversation in which an American desk officer told a Vietnamese official, Thuan, that {q('frus1958-60v01/d135')} ({C('frus1958-60v01/d135')}); at the National Security Council on 9 May the President answered a briefing on criticism that Diem's administration had fostered corruption by saying the United States had been proud of Diem ({C('frus1958-60v01/d156')}); a few days later Ambassador Durbrow carried the complaint about corruption in the Can Lao party to Diem himself, who answered that the party's job was to stop corruption ({C('frus1958-60v01/d160')}). In November 1961 Ambassador Nolting reported that Senator Ellender, visiting Saigon, held that corruption was bred by American assistance ({C('frus1961-63v01/d298')}). In July 1967 the embassy's political counselor agreed that {q('frus1964-68v05/d258',0)} and then refused the remedy of an American veto over provincial and divisional appointments as {q('frus1964-68v05/d258',1)} ({C('frus1964-68v05/d258')}). The Department's August 1967 cable on American leverage after the elections warned that careless use of leverage could undermine the Vietnamese government's self-respect, and an editors' note records that the priorities paper sent with it put corruption third ({C('frus1964-68v05/d301')}). In June 1972 the State Department's William Sullivan told the Washington Special Actions Group that a Senate Foreign Relations Committee staff report said there was corruption throughout South Vietnam and that American officials ignored it, and that the report was expected before Congress recessed ({C('frus1969-76v08/d191')}). The same limit appears in a 1986 White House memorandum on African hunger: {q('frus1981-88v41/d251')} ({C('frus1981-88v41/d251')}).\n")
T.append(f"### 4. The China story became a lesson, and the allegation a retort\n\nIn November 1949 Acheson told the Shah of Iran: {q('frus1949v06/d330',0)} The memorandum of the talk records the reply: {q('frus1949v06/d330',1)} ({C('frus1949v06/d330')}). A year later the retort came from the Shah himself: in an interview with Reuters, reported by the American chargé, he complained that sections of the American press kept accusing Iran of corruption and asked {q('frus1950v05/d289')} ({C('frus1950v05/d289')}). In December 1950, in the Truman-Attlee talks, General Marshall said Chiang had been held free of personal corruption but his followers and party were corrupt, in an argument over recognition and Formosa ({C('frus1950v03/d728')}). Corruption was also a banner others raised: after the 1961 coup in Korea, Assistant Secretary Harriman reported from Tokyo in March 1962 on the new rulers' drive against corruption and, despite a purge law he called a questionable method, recommended {q('frus1961-63v22/d252')} ({C('frus1961-63v22/d252')}).\n")
T.append(f"### 5. From 1975 the briber was often American\n\nIn August 1975 the Department told Secretary Kissinger that publicity about agents' commissions was a problem that could strain American relations with Saudi Arabia: {q('frus1969-76ve09p2/d155')} ({C('frus1969-76ve09p2/d155')}). President Carter then took the matter into diplomacy; at the London summit in May 1977 he asked the other leaders for help against bribery in trade ({C('frus1977-80v03/d35')}), and the record of his June 1977 talk with Venezuela's president says: {q('frus1977-80v24/d337')} ({C('frus1977-80v24/d337')}). The trade side pushed back: in July 1980 the Secretary of Commerce and the Trade Representative told the President that {q('frus1977-80v03/d248')}, while adding that the prohibition was fully intended ({C('frus1977-80v03/d248')}). The earlier summit follow-up and the January 1980 export-policy memorandum are in the collection ({C('frus1977-80v03/d65')}; {C('frus1977-80v03/d230')}).\n")
T.append(f"### 6. Corrupt courts, and the protection of Americans abroad\n\nBefore the Cold War, corruption was a reason for American practice abroad. In 1893 the minister at Constantinople had the investigation of an attack on American missionaries moved away from the local officials after being told that {q('frus1893/d628')} ({C('frus1893/d628')}). At the end of 1921 the American minister in China argued that extraterritoriality (American consular jurisdiction over Americans in China) should not end until China had developed sound judicial habits that would prevent a return to {q('frus1921v01/d275')} ({C('frus1921v01/d275')}).\n")
T.append("## How this was done\n")
T.append("I searched the index's full text (which reduces words to roots, so \"corrupt\" also finds \"corruption\" and \"corrupted\") for a family of corruption words, set aside front matter, editorial notes and the second editions of two volumes, and dated documents by their own dates rather than by volume year. I measured the family by decade per thousand dated documents and by volume, found 3,058 hits, and tested the family on an evenly spaced forty-document sample: every sampled document held an inflected form of the family (40 of 40), 35 of 40 held a basic form, 33 of 40 used the word in the sense of the question and 8 of 40 tied it to an American choice. I then repeated the count of spelling variants in the volumes' own text, where the index's root-reduction cannot blur them. Candidate documents came from proximity searches pairing corruption with aid, loans, supervision, and policy words, and from the neighbours of three anchor documents in the app's meaning-based document vectors; I retrieved each candidate whole and read the passages around the corruption words, not every page. Archival origins come from the document source lines and the bundled archival indexes. I have checked that every quotation in this overview and every excerpt in the collection was verified against its document text in the memo, and that every document cited here is in the collection.\n")
T.append("## Read next\n")
T.append("The collection \"Corruption and American foreign policy, run s5, 2026-10-30\" holds the 38 documents under the six headings above, with a note on each, the passages quoted here, and a closing list of threads not followed. Your additions would unlock the most in three places: the Chinese Nationalist and Philippine cases at the National Archives (Central Files and the Conference Files lot), the Church Subcommittee's papers for the 1975 to 1976 payments, and a decision on whether to extend the set to the Marcos and Noriega years, which lie at the edge of this index.\n")
txt="\n".join(T)
txt=re.sub(r'"([^"\n]+)"',lambda m:"‘"+m.group(1)+"’",txt)
open("overview.md","w").write(txt)
